// Four independent loop-carried chains: a, b, c, d. This is the case unrolling
// exists for, and the reason the pass cannot simply be turned off.
//
// math and mix are both one serial chain -- `s = (s * 31 + i) % d` needs the
// previous iteration's multiply -- so four copies of the body serialise against
// each other exactly as one copy did, and unrolling only adds three more
// compare/branch pairs. Here each of a, b, c, d has its own recurrence, so the
// four updates fill each other's multiply latency and the body gets wider than
// any single chain is long. That is the whole difference between the two shapes,
// and it is a dependence difference, not a size difference: this body is 5
// statements and math's is 2.
//
// The printed sum is not checked against anything here. bench.py only times
// these; the correctness companion is tests/cases/unroll_dep.z.
int main() {
    var a = 1; var b = 2; var c = 3; var d = 4;
    var i = 0;
    while (i < 50000000) {
        a = a * 3 + 1;
        b = b + 2;
        c = c + 3;
        d = d + 4;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    print(a + b + c + d);
    return 0;
}
